Real-World Applications of SoCs
SoCs power many of the devices we use every day — and they’re increasingly being used in futuristic applications too:

Smartphones and Tablets – Apple’s A-series, Qualcomm’s Snapdragon, and Samsung’s Exynos chips are prime examples.
IoT Devices – SoCs with embedded sensors and wireless modules are the backbone of smart home and industrial automation devices.
Automotive Electronics – From digital dashboards to advanced driver-assistance systems (ADAS), cars are packed with SoC-powered electronics.
Edge AI and ML – SoCs like Google’s Edge TPU and NVIDIA Jetson are enabling AI inference at the edge.
Challenges in SoC Design
While SoCs offer immense benefits, they also introduce new design challenges:

Increased Design Complexity – Integrating so many functions onto one chip is not easy. It requires deep cross-domain expertise.
Verification Overhead – Ensuring that every subsystem works correctly and securely adds significant effort and time.
Power and Thermal Management – With so many functions on one chip, power distribution and heat dissipation become critical.
Security Risks – With everything on a single chip, hardware security vulnerabilities can have far-reaching consequences.
